n    depth-$2$ partial return kernel with spectral radius\n    $\\rho(\\tilde{B}_2) = 129/1024 \\approx 0.1260$;\n    per-return information cost\n    $c_0 = \\log_2(1024/129) \\approx 2.989$~bits\n  & GPT (concept) + Claude (formalization + computation) \\\\[3pt]\n57 & \\colorbox{red!25}{Critical}\n  & Bounded invariant core\n    (Prop.~\\ref{prop:bounded-core-main}):\n    $|I_r| = 5$ for all $r \\ge 2$\n    (three fixed points $q = k \\cdot 8^{r-1} - 1$,\n    $k \\in \\{1,4,8\\}$, plus one $2$-cycle);\n    projective limit\n    (Thm.~\\ref{thm:projective-main}):\n    $\\varprojlim I_r = \\{-1\\}$ in $\\mathbb{Z}_8$\n  & Claude (computation + proof) \\\\[3pt]\n58 & \\colorbox{red!25}{Critical}\n  & Absorption Bottleneck Lemma\n    (Lem.~\\ref{lem:bottleneck-main}):\n    channel capacity $\\le \\log_2 5 \\approx 2.322 < c_0$;\n    per-return deficit $0.667$~bits;\n    geometric decay at rate $\\alpha = 645/1024 \\approx 0.630$\n    (Cor.~\\ref{cor:geom-decay-main})\n  & GPT (information-theoretic framing) +\n    Claude (proof + computation) \\\\[3pt]\n59 & \\colorbox{orange!30}{High}\n  & M-value collapse discovery:\n    at $r \\ge 3$, all five $I_r$ elements produce the\n    same post-absorption residue; effective channel\n    capacity drops to zero bits.\n    At $r \\ge 4$, first $n \\equiv 1 \\pmod{8^r}$ coincides\n    with $n = 1$\n  & Claude (computation + diagnosis) \\\\[3pt]\n60 & \\colorbox{red!25}{Critical}\n  & Orbit autonomy diagnosis:\n    orbit $\\bmod\\;8^r$ is \\emph{not} autonomous because\n    $v_2(3n{+}1)$ depends on all digits of $n$;\n    absorption for general~$r$ cannot be proved by\n    finite mod-$8^r$ computation alone.\n    Confirms algebraic reduction has reached its\n    natural fixed point\n  & Claude (analysis) + Moderator (diagnosis) \\\\[3pt]\n61 & \\colorbox{orange!30}{High}\n  & Absorption theorem\n    (Thm.~\\ref{thm:absorption-main}):\n    verified computationally for $r = 2, \\ldots, 10$\n    (max 194 steps); all five $I_r$ elements reach\n    fixed point $1 \\pmod{8^r}$\n  & Claude (computation) \\\\[3pt]\n62 & \\colorbox{orange!30}{High}\n  & Branch anti-concentration reduction\n    (Prop.~\\ref{prop:branch-perm-main}):\n    $\\gcd(9,8) = 1$ makes fresh-digit map a permutation\n    within each return branch; no algebraic amplification;\n    concentration must come from branch selection\n    (Cor.~\\ref{cor:conc-source-main})\n  & GPT (two-step proposal) + Claude (proof) \\\\[3pt]\n63 & \\colorbox{orange!30}{High}\n  & Gap-induced decorrelation (empirical):\n    $3000$ orbits, $5000$ steps each;\n    $\\chi^2$ test: gap~$> 50$ gives $p = 0.18$\n    (no significant correlation);\n    gap~$\\le 5$ gives $p < 10^{-10}$\n    (strong correlation).\n    Diagnosis: proving $\\beta_g \\to 0$ algebraically\n    is equivalent to Collatz mixing\n  & Claude (computation + diagnosis) \\\\[3pt]\n64 & \\colorbox{orange!30}{High}\n  & Path-conditional bijection\n    (Thm.~\\ref{thm:path-bijection-main}):\n    for every valuation path~$\\pi$, fresh-digit map\n    is a bijection ($3^k$ coprime to $q$);\n    closes annealed anti-concentration argument\n  & Claude (proof) \\\\[3pt]\n65 & \\colorbox{orange!30}{High}\n  & Memory lower bound\n    (Thm.~\\ref{thm:memory-main}):\n    sustaining a chain of length~$N$ requires\n    $\\ge N \\cdot c_0$ bits of memory.\n    Theorem ladder: (A)~fixed-$k$ impossibility proved,\n    (B)~memory lower bound proved,\n    (C)~orbit memory upper bound open\n    ($c' \\le 0.51$ empirically, $6\\times$ below $c_0$)\n  & Claude (proof + computation) \\\\[3pt]\n66 & \\colorbox{red!25}{Critical}\n  & Single-hypothesis reduction:\n    Collatz conjecture reduced to information-rate\n    bound $c' < c_0$ on $5$-element set\n    (Conj.~\\ref{conj:info-rate});\n    separation of concerns table\n    (Table~\\ref{tab:separation-summary});\n    ``the maze is mapped, the walls are known''\n  & Moderator (framing) + GPT (positioning) +\n    Claude (formalization) \\\\[3pt]\n67 & \\colorbox{yellow!50}{Med}\n  & Three-way relay methodology (v3):\n    GPT as structural proposer, Moderator as\n    strategic evaluator, Claude as rigorous verifier;\n    evolution from two-party to three-party collaboration\n  & Moderator (orchestration) \\\\[3pt]\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\\begin{table}[!ht]\n\\centering\n\\scriptsize\n\\caption{Contribution attribution (Part 5 of 40): v4 return structure and operator identification.}\n\\label{tab:contributions-5}\n\\renewcommand{\\arraystretch}{1.0}\n\\begin{tabular}{@{}clp{8.8cm}p{4.0cm}@{}}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{\\#} & \\textbf{Level} & \\textbf{Contribution} & \\textbf{Primary source} \\\\\n\\midrule\n68 & \\colorbox{orange!30}{High}\n  & $q \\equiv 7$ two-step return theorem\n    (Prop.~\\ref{prop:q7-return}):\n    for $q = 8m + 7$, the fiber-57 orbit returns in\n    exactly two Syracuse steps with quotient\n    $q' = 9m + 8$; the return is perfectly uniform\n    when $m \\bmod 8$ is uniform\n  & Claude (algebraic proof) + GPT (cross-validation) \\\\[3pt]\n69 & \\colorbox{orange!30}{High}\n  & $q \\equiv 3$ minimum return-gap theorem\n    (Prop.~\\ref{prop:q3-gap}):\n    for $q = 8m + 3$, the orbit cannot return to\n    fiber~57 in fewer than five Syracuse steps;\n    each intermediate residue $\\bmod\\;64$ is\n    algebraically determined and $\\neq 57$\n  & Claude (algebraic proof + verification) \\\\[3pt]\n70 & \\colorbox{orange!30}{High}\n  & Kernel decomposition remark:\n    fiber-57 returns split into a fast uniform branch\n    ($q \\equiv 7$, 14.4\\%, 2-step) and a slow\n    sparse branch ($q \\equiv 3$, 11.0\\%,\n    $\\ge 5$-step with 9 new low-order bits),\n    explaining why even short orbits mix quickly\n  & GPT (kernel-split concept) +\n    Claude (computation + formalization) \\\\[3pt]\n71 & \\colorbox{orange!30}{High}\n  & Invariant core rigidity remark:\n    chain map $q \\mapsto 9q + 8 \\bmod 8^r$ acts as a\n    permutation on $I_r$ at every depth (three fixed\n    points plus one 2-c